    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d449">

                <pb facs="0459" n="447" xml:id="pg447"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00119 EW/5–445: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in France</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Caffery</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Extracts]<anchor n="77" xml:id="d449-f1"/>
                    </seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Paris</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-05-04T14:00:00+02:00">May 4, 1945—2
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 10:07 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>2345. For Matthews<anchor n="78" xml:id="d449-f2"/> from Murphy.<anchor n="78a"
                        xml:id="d449-f3"/>
                </p>
                <p rendition="#center">. . . . . . .</p>
                <p>Regarding Czechoslovakia the British of course are pressing for continued advance
                    into that country and it looks as if we may advance up to the line of the
                        Moldau<anchor n="79" xml:id="d449-f4"/> for operational reasons. British
                    reasoning explains their proposals for a civil affairs agreement with the Czechs
                    which they have been promoting. It should be noted that the proposed agreement
                    is between the Supreme Commander and the Czech Government and not between the
                    Czechs and the United States and British Governments. [Murphy.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Caffery</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d449-f1" n="77" xml:id="d449-f1-content">
                <p>For remainder of this telegram, see <ref target="frus1945v03#pg_775">vol. <hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">iii</hi>, p. 775</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d449-f2" n="78" xml:id="d449-f2-content">
                <p>H. Freeman Matthews, Director of the Office of European Affairs.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d449-f3" n="78a" xml:id="d449-f3-content">
                <p>Robert D. Murphy, U.S. Political Adviser for Germany.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d449-f4" n="79" xml:id="d449-f4-content">
                <p>In Czech, the Vltava River.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
